This behavior is a common UI "focus" bug in Material UI (MUI) dialogs. When a user clicks the "backdrop" (the darkened area outside the dialog), the browser doesn't know where to move the focus, so it sometimes "sticks" the focus state on the last active element—or worse, it incorrectly triggers a :focus or :hover style on a button near the edge of the dialog.
Here is how to fix this for your VoucherPosting.jsx or VoucherBulkUpload.jsx components.
### The Fix: Control the Backdrop Focus
The most common cause is the disableAutoFocus or keepMounted properties in your <Dialog> component (or the Modal/Popover used in your custom implementation).
#### 1. Add disableRestoreFocus
If your button is getting highlighted when clicking away, add the disableRestoreFocus prop to your Dialog component. This prevents the dialog from "giving back" the focus to the last clicked button when it closes or loses focus.
```jsx
<Dialog
  open={isOpen}
  onClose={handleClose}
  disableRestoreFocus={true} // Add this
  // ... other props
>

```
#### 2. Fix the CSS :focus Outline
If the button is literally changing color (like getting a blue border or a hover effect) when you click outside, it is a CSS :focus state. You can override this in your VoucherStyle.js or VoucherBulkStyle.js:
```javascript
// Add this to your style file for the button
const buttonStyle = {
  // ... existing styles
  "&:focus": {
    outline: "none", // Removes the browser's default highlight
    boxShadow: "none", // Removes the glow effect
  },
};

```
### Why this happens (The Technical Reason)
When you click the backdrop:
 1. **The Event Bubbles:** The click event hits the backdrop.
 2. **Focus Trap:** MUI uses a "Focus Trap" to keep the user inside the dialog. When you click outside, the trap tries to calculate the next "logical" element to focus on.
 3. **The "Ghost" Hover:** If your button is positioned near the edge of the dialog, and you click the backdrop right next to it, the browser might be incorrectly calculating that the mouse is still "hovering" over the button area because the dialog didn't re-render fast enough to register the backdrop click.
### How to test if this fixed it
 1. Open your browser **Inspect Element** (F12).
 2. Go to the **Elements** tab.
 3. Click the **":hov"** button in the top right of the Styles pane.
 4. Check the :focus or :focus-visible boxes.
 5. If you see the button change style, you know exactly which CSS class is causing the highlight.
**If you are using a custom Tooltip or Popover instead of a standard MUI Dialog:**
You might also need to add disablePortal={true} to your button components. Sometimes the "portal" (the way MUI renders elements outside the normal DOM tree) causes these focus calculation errors when you click the backdrop.
Apply disableRestoreFocus={true} first—it is the solution for 90% of "button highlights when clicking away" issues in MUI!
